<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Švitrigaila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–<a href="/years/1452">1452</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Svidrigailo, Świdrygiełło · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Švitrigaila was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> and Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1430 to 1432. Supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Ruthenian nobles, he was overthrown by Sigismund Kęstutaitis and fought a civil war until his defeat at Wiłkomierz in 1435. He later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> as duke until his death in 1452, after which it was turned into a voivodeship of the Grand Duchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svitrigaila.jpg" alt="Švitrigaila" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Guagnini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svitrigaila_(118503655).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Švitrigaila was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a>, Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and his second wife, Uliana of Tver. He first appears in politics in October 1382, as a witness to the Treaty of Dubysa between his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. In 1386 he was baptised in the Roman Catholic rite at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> with the name Bolesław. Before his death Algirdas passed his throne to Jogaila, but made him swear to make Švitrigaila his heir. Jogaila's representatives later claimed the arrangement had been modified by mutual agreement between the brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1392 Švitrigaila rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> after Jogaila appointed a regent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, but his cousin Vytautas and Skirgaila captured him and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. His later life was largely a series of unsuccessful struggles against Vytautas. He sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, the long-standing enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and from Sigismund of Luxemburg. The Pact of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> of January 1401 confirmed Vytautas as grand duke and ended Švitrigaila's hope of that title, and in 1402 he went to Marienburg and made a treaty with the Knights.</p><p>In May 1409 he defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where Vasily I gave him several towns. The standoff on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> ended in a peace, and Švitrigaila returned to Vytautas's court. He conspired again with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, was arrested and was held for nine years, until his rescue from Kremenets Castle on the night of 24 March 1418. He reconciled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> in 1419 and with Vytautas in August 1420, and afterwards served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gollub-war">Gollub War</a> and on a mission to Riga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Vytautas died in October 1430, Lithuanian nobles elected Švitrigaila grand duke without the Polish approval promised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> of 1413. To win Ruthenian votes he gave Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> nobles equal rights, one lasting achievement of his brief reign. Polish nobles under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki">Zbigniew Oleśnicki</a> demanded that he acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, and he refused.</p><p>On the night of 31 August 1432 conspirators led by Sigismund Kęstutaitis attacked him at Ashmyany. He escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, and Sigismund became grand duke. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> divided between Sigismund's western supporters and Švitrigaila's supporters in the east, including Polotsk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. Three years of fighting ended with Sigismund's victory at Wiłkomierz in 1435.</p><p>Sigismund was assassinated in 1440 by nobles who supported Švitrigaila, who returned to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. The Council of Lords elected Casimir Jagiellon grand duke that June. Švitrigaila died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> on 10 February 1452 and was buried at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kiev Pechersk Lavra</a>. Shortly before his death he bequeathed his possessions in Podolia and Volhynia to the Lithuanian state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Švitrigaila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later <b>Svidrigaila</b>, a son of Algirdas, ruled it from the 1430s after losing the struggle for the grand ducal throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-volhynia">Duchy of Volhynia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Lutsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.75,25.32&amp;year=1423" aria-label="Lutsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5127 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Švitrigaila"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5577 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5577 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.75,25.32&amp;year=1423">What was here in 1423? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
